# DEFECT 076, the string's half: a runtime line that blamed the wrong thing
# (M-agreed-retention, 2026-09-23).
#
# `clobber_before` writes over the eight bytes before the text it is lent, which
# is where a `str`'s block keeps its mark. The program prints the word, and the
# check that runs when the word's last reference goes finds the mark gone.
#
# **The line it printed until this case was written was false.** It said *a str
# was fabricated from a foreign pointer; use hero_str_from_bytes*: one cause,
# stated as the only one, and a runtime function no Heroes program can call.
# Nothing here was fabricated. The same check was reached 8 runs in 10 on
# Darwin and 5 in 5 on Linux x86-64 by a false `owned` on a cell C had already
# freed, which is the shape that found the defect; that one depends on the heap
# and cannot pin anything, so this case pins the class with a writer that
# reaches the check on every run.
#
# **What the case asserts** is the first sentence of the new line, the fact the
# runtime holds: the mark is gone. The causes follow it as causes, which is
# panel 173 R1's rule, *no path prints a sentence measured false*.

extern "fixedbugs-c-writes-over-a-strings-header.h"
    function clobber_before(s: cstr lent)

function main()
    word = repeat("ab", 4)
    clobber_before(s: word.cstr())
    print(word)
